SteamARM: a relative pointer for mouse-look (patches/xquartz-pointer-confine.patch
holds the pointer; this gives games its motion on relative X/Y axes).

While a game holds the pointer (a grab with confine_to and the cursor hidden),
X11Application.m keeps the macOS cursor at the window's centre, dissociated
from the mouse, and the mouse's motion reached X clients only as the Rel X /
Rel Y valuators (2, 3) of the "xquartz virtual pointer", whose axes 0 and 1
are absolute. SDL 3 reads Rel X / Rel Y, but Wine reads only axes 0 and 1
(winex11.drv mouse.c update_relative_valuators), for raw input (WM_INPUT) and
for the cursor while it is clipped: they stood still at the centre, so
Windows games under Proton (Schedule I, Unity) could not turn the camera.

A second slave pointer, "xquartz relative pointer", with relative X/Y axes
(0, 1, labelled Rel X / Rel Y) and its own scroll axes (2, 3), carries the
motion, buttons and scroll while the pointer is held; the slave switch tells
clients (XI_DeviceChanged, XISlaveSwitch) that the axes changed, which Wine
and SDL 3 both follow. Free pointer: the virtual pointer, as before.
